# 8. Functional Requirements

Functional requirements define what the system must do.

## FR-01: Student Registration and Authentication

The platform must allow students to create accounts and access the system securely.

**Requirements:**

- Users can register using their name, email, phone number, and password.
- Initial access is restricted to eligible FAST-NUCES Chiniot Campus students.
- Institutional email verification should be supported where available.
- Additional student verification may be performed through an uploaded student ID or administrative approval.
- Users must complete basic profile information.
- Users can log in and log out.
- Users can reset forgotten passwords.
- Users can update their personal information.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- An unverified account cannot access restricted ride-booking functionality.
- A verified student can access the platform.
- Duplicate accounts using the same registered email must be prevented.
- Users can recover access through a secure password-reset process.

## FR-02: Student Profile Management

Each registered user must have a profile.

**Profile fields:**
- Full name.
- Profile photograph.
- University.
- Campus.
- Department.
- Semester.
- Student verification status.
- Contact information.
- Preferred commuting times.
- Home area or general pickup locality.
- Average user rating.

Contact details should not be publicly exposed without appropriate consent.

## FR-03: Driver and Transport Registration

The platform must allow eligible users to register a transportation arrangement.

**Registration information:**

- Transportation category: Personal vehicle or existing transport service.
- Vehicle type.
- Vehicle registration number, where applicable.
- Vehicle model.
- Passenger capacity.
- Available passenger seats.
- Driver name.
- Driver contact information.
- General route.
- Departure location.
- Destination.
- Departure time.
- Return time.
- Daily estimated travel expense.

**Additional requirements:**

- Vehicle owners may register their own vehicles.
- Transport coordinators may register an existing service only when authorized to do so.
- Driver and vehicle information must be distinguishable from the account holder.
- Administrators can review and verify submitted transportation information.
- Users can update their availability.

## FR-04: Ride Publishing

A verified ride provider must be able to publish a ride.

**Ride information:**

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| Starting location | Chiniot or another supported pickup location |
| Destination | Faisalabad / campus |
| Travel date | Specific date |
| Departure time | Planned departure |
| Return time | Planned return |
| Vehicle | Registered vehicle or transport arrangement |
| Available seats | Number of seats offered |
| Estimated total cost | Expected trip expense |
| Cost-sharing method | Equal division or agreed contribution |
| Additional notes | Pickup instructions and conditions |

Ride providers should be able to publish:

- One-time rides.
- Daily recurring availability.
- Selected weekdays.
- Return journeys.
- Separate morning and evening rides.

A recurring ride must generate manageable ride occurrences so that daily availability and cancellations can be handled independently.

## FR-05: Ride Discovery and Search

Passengers must be able to search for rides.

**Search filters:**

- Starting location.
- Destination.
- Travel date.
- Departure time.
- Available seats.
- Vehicle category.
- Estimated per-person cost.
- Morning or evening journey.

Search results should display:

- Ride provider's name and verification status.
- Profile rating.
- Vehicle type.
- Starting and destination areas.
- Departure time.
- Available seats.
- Estimated individual cost.
- Return availability.
- Ride details.

Passengers should be able to view full ride information before requesting a seat.

## FR-06: Ride Request and Booking

Passengers must be able to request seats on available rides.

**Booking workflow:**

1. Passenger discovers a suitable ride.
2. Passenger opens ride details.
3. Passenger selects the number of seats required.
4. Passenger submits a ride request.
5. Ride provider receives a notification.
6. Ride provider accepts or rejects the request.
7. The passenger receives the booking status.
8. Upon acceptance, the seat is reserved.
9. Both parties can coordinate through chat.

**Booking statuses:**

- Pending.
- Accepted.
- Rejected.
- Cancelled.
- Completed.
- No-show.

**Business rules:**

- A booking cannot exceed available seat capacity.
- Pending requests must not be treated as confirmed seats.
- Confirmed bookings must reserve seats transactionally to prevent overbooking.
- Users must be able to cancel according to the applicable cancellation policy.
- Providers must be notified of booking changes.

## FR-07: Timetable Upload and Schedule Management

Students should be able to upload their academic timetables to help identify compatible commuting schedules.

**Supported formats:**
- PDF.
- JPG.
- PNG.

The system should also provide manual schedule entry.

**Timetable information:**
- Day of the week.
- Class start time.
- Class end time.
- Campus.
- Optional break periods.

**Expected functionality:**

- Students can upload or update their timetables.
- Students can manually specify preferred departure and return times.
- The platform can identify overlapping commuting availability.
- Students can receive suggestions for compatible rides.
- Users can change their commuting preferences without uploading a new timetable.

Timetable extraction using OCR or AI is optional for the MVP. Manual confirmation must be available if automatic extraction is introduced.

## FR-08: Route-Based Ride Matching

The platform must suggest potential carpools based on compatible travel information.

**Matching inputs:**

1. Starting location.
2. Destination.
3. Travel date.
4. Departure time.
5. Return time.
6. Available seats.
7. User timetable.
8. General pickup and drop-off areas.

**Initial matching logic:**

- Match users travelling in the same direction.
- Prioritize similar departure times.
- Consider nearby pickup locations.
- Consider compatible return schedules.
- Exclude rides with no remaining seats.
- Exclude cancelled or unavailable rides.

**Suggested matching priority:**

| Matching factor | Suggested weight |
|---|---:|
| Route compatibility | 40% |
| Departure time compatibility | 25% |
| Pickup location proximity | 20% |
| Return schedule compatibility | 15% |
| Total | 100% |

The weights are initial product assumptions and should be adjusted using real commuting data.

The platform should present ranked suggestions rather than automatically booking rides.

## FR-09: Daily Cost-Sharing Calculator

The platform must calculate and display the estimated individual commuting expense.

**Primary formula:**

Per-person cost = Total daily trip cost ÷ Number of participating passengers

**Example:**

Assume the total daily commuting cost is PKR 1,000.

| Passengers | Cost per passenger |
|---:|---:|
| 1 | PKR 1,000 |
| 2 | PKR 500 |
| 3 | PKR 333.33 |
| 4 | PKR 250 |
| 5 | PKR 200 |

The calculation should use the actual number of participating passengers, including the driver only if the group explicitly agrees to include the driver in the cost split.

**Cost-sharing rules:**

- The total cost is entered by the ride provider.
- The system calculates an estimated individual share.
- The cost should update when confirmed passenger numbers change.
- The final amount must be visible before the ride is confirmed.
- Users must agree on the contribution before travelling.
- The platform must not automatically collect money in the MVP.
- Cash is exchanged directly between participants.

The system must clearly distinguish between an estimated cost and an agreed final contribution.

## FR-10: In-App Messaging

Users must be able to communicate within the platform.

**Messaging features:**

- One-to-one conversations between ride providers and passengers.
- Conversation access after a ride request.
- Text messages.
- Pickup location coordination.
- Departure time confirmation.
- Booking-related communication.
- Basic message timestamps.
- Reporting and blocking functionality.

The platform should avoid exposing personal phone numbers publicly.

## FR-11: Notifications

The system must notify users about important activities.

**Notification events:**

- Successful registration.
- Student verification.
- New ride request.
- Request acceptance or rejection.
- Ride cancellation.
- Departure time changes.
- New chat messages.
- Upcoming confirmed rides.
- Changes in available seats.
- Ride completion reminders.

MVP notifications should support in-app notifications and email where appropriate. Push notifications may be introduced depending on the chosen platform.

## FR-12: Reviews and Ratings

Students should be able to review completed rides.

**Review information:**
- Overall rating.
- Optional written feedback.
- Punctuality.
- Communication.
- Ride experience.

**Rules:**
- Only users with eligible completed bookings can submit reviews.
- Users cannot review the same booking multiple times.
- Administrators can investigate inappropriate reviews.
- Ratings should be linked to verified platform activity.

## FR-13: Cancellation Management

The platform must support cancellations by both passengers and ride providers.

**Requirements:**

- Passengers can cancel confirmed bookings.
- Providers can cancel rides.
- Other passengers must receive notifications when a ride is cancelled.
- Cancelled seats should become available again where applicable.
- Cancellation history should be maintained.
- Repeated cancellations may be flagged for administrative review.

## FR-14: Reporting and Safety

Users must be able to report suspicious accounts, inappropriate behavior, or transportation-related concerns.

**Report categories:**
- Fake profile.
- Incorrect vehicle information.
- Misleading ride details.
- Harassment.
- Unsafe driving concerns.
- No-show.
- Unapproved fare changes.
- Other misconduct.

Administrators must be able to review reports and take appropriate action.

